{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>If the sun has risen upon him.</b> This is nothing but a kind of parable: if the matter is clear to you that he comes in peace with you — like this sun, which is peace in the world — so too it is plain to you that he does not come to kill, even if the owner of the property stands against him; for example, a father tunneling to steal the son's property — it is known that the father has mercy on the son and does not come for bloodshed (ibid.)." "<b>He has bloodguilt.</b> He is accounted as a living person, and it is murder if the master of the house kills him." "<b>He shall surely make restitution.</b> The thief [restores] the property he stole, for which he is not liable to death; and Onkelos, who rendered ״if the eye of witnesses fell upon him,״ took a different approach, to say that if witnesses found him before the master of the house came, and when the master of the house came against him they warned him not to kill him — <b>he has bloodguilt</b>: he is liable for it if he killed him, since once there are witnesses for him, this thief does not come for bloodshed, and [the owner] must not kill the owner of the property."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:47.504916338Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}